===Etymology 2===
From Late {{inh|de|gmh|schern|t=to hurry, to escape}}, first attested in the 15th century.<ref>Wolfgang Pfeifer: ''[https://www.dwds.de/wb/scheren#et-1 Etymologisches Wörterbuch.]''</ref> Of {{unc|de|nocap=1}} origin. Possibly from {{inh|de|goh|scerōn|t=to be exuberant; to rest}}. If so ultimately from {{der|de|ine-pro|*(s)kerd-||to move, sway, swing, jump}}. See also {{cog|goh|scerdo||hinge}}, {{cog|la|cardō||hinge}}, {{cog|lt|kárti||to hang}}.<ref>{{R:ine:EIEC|324}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{de-verb|<haben,sein>}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive|rare|_|outside compounds}} to go into a certain direction
# {{lb|de|reflexive|informal}} to [[scram]]; to [[beat it]]
# {{lb|de|transitive|geometry}} to [[shear]], to [[apply]] a [[shearing]] [[transformation]] to {{gloss|displacing each point of a geometric figure in fixed direction by an amount proportional to its signed distance, thus preserving the area of the figure.}}
#: {{uxi|de|ein Rechteck zu einem Parallelogramm '''scheren'''|'''to shear''' a rectangle into a parallelogram}}
